This directory contains a MODIFIED copy of SingleFile CLI.

  Upstream project : SingleFile CLI (single-file-cli)
  Upstream author  : Gildas Lormeau
  Upstream source  : https://github.com/gildas-lormeau/single-file-cli
  Upstream version : 2.0.83 (commit c153d9613d50cabd5ba356c79d2b28b12b887236)
  Licence          : GNU Affero General Public License v3.0 - see LICENSE

SingleFile is the work of Gildas Lormeau and contributors. It is redistributed
here under the AGPL-3.0, which is why this repository as a whole is AGPL-3.0
rather than MIT. The directory was renamed for use inside this project; that
rename does not change the authorship of anything in it.

Section 5(a) of the AGPL requires a modified version to carry prominent notices
stating that it was changed. The changes made to the upstream sources are:

  1. Every `.js` source file was renamed to `.mjs`, and every relative import
     was updated to match. SingleFile is ESM and declared that through
     `"type": "module"` in its package.json; this copy has no package.json, so
     the extension has to carry the module type instead.

  2. `lib/cdp-client.mjs` had its bare `simple-cdp` import rewritten to the
     relative path `../../simple-cdp/mod.mjs`. The bare specifier required a
     `node_modules` lookup, which this project no longer has.

  3. `single-file-cli-api.js` was renamed to `capture-api.mjs`, and the
     reference to it in `single-file-launcher.mjs` was updated.

  4. Files not needed to run the capture engine in-process were not copied:
     package.json, package-lock.json, deno.json, deno.lock, eslint.config.mjs,
     Dockerfile, the build/compile shell scripts, the CLI shell and batch
     wrappers, the .github workflow, and resources/single-file.ico.

  5. `capture-api.mjs` releases a task's `promise` and `options` once the task
     reaches the "processed" state, and looks up already-queued URLs through a
     Set instead of Array.includes. Every change is marked with a
     `web-archiver:` comment at the site.

     Upstream drives one process per invocation, so the task list living until
     the process exits costs nothing. This project keeps a single engine alive
     for a whole crawl and calls capture() once per batch, which turns that
     into an unbounded leak: a resolved task promise pins its pageData, and
     pageData is the complete captured document with every resource already
     inlined as base64. A few hundred pages was enough to exhaust the heap. The
     Set is the same situation for time rather than memory - the linear scan
     was O(batch x run) per batch.

Other than the above, no source line was altered. Diffing this directory
against upstream v2.0.83, after undoing the renames in items 1-3, shows only
the change described in item 5.
